  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "Finding Output with $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the time-domain output of a continuous-time LTI system by forming $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$, simplifying the Laplace-domain product, and applying the inverse Laplace transform.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Finding Output with $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Finding Output with $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The core move of this section is to compute the output of a continuous-time LTI system by following the fixed flow: form $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$, simplify the Laplace-domain product, and then invert once to get $y(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The required objects are the input transform $X(s)$, the transfer function $H(s)$, and the target time-domain output $y(t)$. They connect through $Y(s)=\\mathcal{L}\\{y(t)\\}$, then $y(t)=\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\{Y(s)\\}$ at the end of the section.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use a base case with first-order factors only: set $X(s)=\\frac{1}{s+3}$ and $H(s)=\\frac{2}{s+4}$. Then form $Y(s)=\\frac{2}{(s+3)(s+4)}$ and identify this as the immediate inverse-transform target before moving to harder algebra.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Checkpoint before inversion: decide whether $Y(s)$ is already in a table match or whether it needs extra algebra such as expansion or partial-fraction setup. Keep this decision explicit so every section follows the same action sequence.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=X(s)H(s)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\{Y(s)\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Finding Output with $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Forming $Y(s)$ from $X(s)$ and $H(s)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compute $Y(s)$ for $X(s)=1/(s+3)$ and $H(s)=4/(s+1)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the product rule for an LTI system in the Laplace domain: form $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$ before doing any inverse-transform work.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=X(s)H(s)=\\left(\\frac{1}{s+3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{4}{s+1}\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Multiply the numerators together and multiply the denominator factors together. The numerator is $1\\cdot 4=4$, and the denominator keeps both first-order factors, $(s+3)(s+1)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=\\frac{4}{(s+3)(s+1)}=\\frac{4}{(s+1)(s+3)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the formed output transform is $Y(s)=\\frac{4}{(s+1)(s+3)}$. Its pole factors are $s+1$ and $s+3$, giving poles at $s=-1$ and $s=-3$ for the later inverse-transform step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This exact rational product is not yet a single basic table-match form. The important result for this step is the correctly formed product; any algebraic decomposition belongs to the next step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Finding Output with $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Simplifying the Laplace-Domain Product",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the simplified algebraic form of $Y(s)$ for $X(s)=\\frac{2s+4}{s+1}$ and $H(s)=\\frac{s+3}{s+2}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is still $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$, but now each factor is more complex than the first example, so this step focuses on algebraic cleanup while preserving the one-notation flow.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=X(s)H(s)=\\left(\\frac{2s+4}{s+1}\\right)\\left(\\frac{s+3}{s+2}\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Multiply the two numerators to get $(2s+4)(s+3)$ and multiply the two denominators to get $(s+1)(s+2)$, keeping numerator and denominator structure explicit.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(2s+4)(s+3)=2s^2+10s+12, \\quad (s+1)(s+2)=s^2+3s+2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=\\frac{(2s+4)(s+3)}{(s+1)(s+2)}=\\frac{2s^2+10s+12}{s^2+3s+2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The simplified result is $Y(s)=\\frac{2s^2+10s+12}{s^2+3s+2}$, equivalent to the fully factorized ratio $Y(s)=\\frac{(2s+4)(s+3)}{(s+1)(s+2)}$. This form is now clean and ready for the next checkpoint: decide whether this is a direct table match or whether partial fractions are needed before inversion.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Finding Output with $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Finding $y(t)$ When $Y(s)$ Matches a Known Transform Pair",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "From $Y(s)=\\frac{5}{s+4}$, derive $y(t)$ directly.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a single first-order transform factor: $Y(s)$ is already in a direct inversion shape, so the move is to identify the matching table entry and map constants.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=\\frac{5}{s+4}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the pair $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-at}u(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s+a}$ and compare directly with $Y(s)=\\frac{K}{s+a}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here, the denominator has $a=4$ and the numerator gives $K=5$, so this is the exact form of a single-term decaying exponential with no extra decomposition.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=\\frac{K}{s+a}\\Longrightarrow y(t)=Ke^{-at}u(t),\\quad a=4,\\;K=5",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=5e^{-4t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "State result and checkpoint: if a later $Y(s)$ has multiple factors or repeated/complex poles, skip the direct table shortcut and route to the partial-fraction or decomposition route.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Finding Output with $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Finding $y(t)$ When $Y(s)$ Requires Partial Fractions",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $y(t)$ for $Y(s)=\\frac{2s+9}{(s+2)(s+5)}$ using partial fractions.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a rational $Y(s)$ with two distinct poles, so direct table inversion is not immediate and the controlled next move is partial fraction decomposition.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Set up the decomposition as $Y(s)=\\frac{A}{s+2}+\\frac{B}{s+5}$ and keep a stable sign convention with positive $a$ in terms of $s+ a$ factors.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{2s+9}{(s+2)(s+5)}=\\frac{A}{s+2}+\\frac{B}{s+5}=\\frac{A(s+5)+B(s+2)}{(s+2)(s+5)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "2s+9=A(s+5)+B(s+2)= (A+B)s+(5A+2B)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Match coefficients to get $A+B=2$ and $5A+2B=9$, so $A=\\frac{5}{3}$ and $B=\\frac{1}{3}$. The inverse-ready form is now ready.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=\\frac{5/3}{s+2}+\\frac{1/3}{s+5}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y(t)=\\frac{5}{3}e^{-2t}u(t)+\\frac{1}{3}e^{-5t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The poles are at $s=-2$ and $s=-5$, both in the left half-plane, so this output is a stable sum of two decaying exponentials.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
